@startuml typical_arch_layers
allowmixing
'
' A TYPICAL layered / clean architecture — the building blocks and where
' they live.  Generic (not this project), for learning the vocabulary.
'
' THE GOLDEN RULE: dependencies point DOWNWARD / INWARD only.
'   Presentation -> API -> Application -> Domain
'   Infrastructure also depends INWARD on Domain (implements its interfaces).
'   Domain depends on NOTHING.  That is what makes it "clean".
'

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam classBackgroundColor #fafafa
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam linetype polyline
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11

title Typical architecture — layers (top = outside world, bottom = pure core)

' ===================== PRESENTATION =====================
package "1 · PRESENTATION  (what the user sees)" #E8F5E9 {
    class "UI / Client\n(browser, mobile, desktop app)" as UI {
        renders screens
        sends HTTP requests
        shows responses
    }
}

' ===================== API / DELIVERY =====================
package "2 · API / DELIVERY  (the front door)" #E3F2FD {
    class "Router / Controller" as Router <<adapter>> {
        maps HTTP route -> a method call
        NO business logic
    }
    class "Request DTO" as ReqDTO <<DTO>> {
        shape of incoming JSON
        (validated at the edge)
    }
    class "Response DTO" as RespDTO <<DTO>> {
        shape of outgoing JSON
        (never leaks DB rows)
    }
    class "Middleware\n(auth, logging, errors)" as Middleware <<cross-cutting>>
}

' ===================== APPLICATION / SERVICE =====================
package "3 · APPLICATION / SERVICE  (orchestrates a use case)" #E0F7FA {
    class "Service / Use-Case" as Service <<service>> {
        validate + coordinate steps
        calls repos + domain + other services
        owns the 'how' of a feature
    }
    class "Mapper / Assembler" as Mapper <<mapper>> {
        DTO  <->  Entity
        keeps the two worlds separate
    }
}

' ===================== DOMAIN / CORE =====================
package "4 · DOMAIN / CORE  (pure business — no tech)" #FFF8E1 {
    class "Entity / Domain Model" as Entity <<entity>> {
        the business 'noun': Order, RenderJob
        identity + business rules + behavior
    }
    class "Value Object" as VO <<value object>> {
        immutable, no identity
        Money, FrameRange, DispatchContext
    }
    interface "Repository INTERFACE\n(a 'Port')" as RepoPort <<port>> {
        save(entity)
        get_by_id(id) : Entity
    }
    interface "External Port\n(e.g. IPaymentGateway)" as ExtPort <<port>>
    class "Domain Service" as DomainSvc <<domain service>> {
        rules that span entities
        (pure functions)
    }
}

' ===================== INFRASTRUCTURE =====================
package "5 · INFRASTRUCTURE  (where data physically lives)" #FFF3E0 {
    class "Repository IMPL" as RepoImpl <<adapter>> {
        implements Repository INTERFACE
        writes/reads the DB
    }
    class "External Client / Adapter" as ExtClient <<adapter>> {
        implements External Port
        calls a 3rd-party API
    }
    database "Database" as DB
    database "Cache (Redis)" as Cache
    cloud "3rd-party API" as ExtAPI
}

' ---------- the dependency flow (top -> down) ----------
UI       --> Router       : HTTP request
Router   ..> ReqDTO       : parses into
Router   ..> RespDTO      : returns
Router   --> Middleware   : passes through
Router   --> Service      : calls (plain method)
Service  --> Mapper       : convert DTO <-> Entity
Service  --> Entity       : uses / mutates
Service  --> DomainSvc    : applies rules
Service  --> RepoPort     : save / load  (depends on the INTERFACE)
Mapper   ..> Entity
Mapper   ..> ReqDTO

' ---------- dependency INVERSION (the clever bit) ----------
RepoImpl  ..|> RepoPort   : implements
ExtClient ..|> ExtPort    : implements
RepoImpl  --> DB
RepoImpl  --> Cache
ExtClient --> ExtAPI

note right of RepoPort
  **Dependency Inversion**
  The domain declares WHAT it needs
  (the interface / port).  Infrastructure
  provides HOW (the impl).  So the arrow
  points INWARD: infra depends on domain,
  never the reverse.  Swap the DB and the
  domain never notices.
end note

note as RULE #FFE0E0
  **THE GOLDEN RULE — dependencies point inward**
  1 Presentation  ->  2 API  ->  3 Application  ->  4 Domain
                       5 Infrastructure  ->  4 Domain
  The DOMAIN (4) imports nothing technical.
  Everything depends on it; it depends on no one.
end note

@enduml


' ##########################################################################
' SEQUENCE — one request flowing DOWN the layers and the response back UP
' ##########################################################################

@startuml typical_arch_flow
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
title One request, end to end — "create an Order" (generic example)

actor User
participant "UI / Client" as UI
participant "Router\n(API layer)" as Router
participant "Request DTO" as DTO
participant "OrderService\n(Application)" as Service
participant "Mapper" as Mapper
participant "Order Entity\n(Domain)" as Entity
participant "OrderRepository\n(interface = Port)" as RepoPort
participant "OrderRepositoryImpl\n(Infrastructure)" as RepoImpl
database "Database" as DB

== request travels DOWN (outside -> inside) ==
User ->> UI : fills form, clicks Submit
UI ->> Router : POST /orders  { items, address }
activate Router
Router -> DTO : validate & parse JSON\ninto CreateOrderRequest
note right of DTO
  **DTO = Data Transfer Object.**
  A dumb data bag matching the WIRE
  shape. NOT a domain object — it has
  no business rules, just fields.
end note
Router -> Service : create_order(dto)
activate Service

== application coordinates the use case ==
Service -> Mapper : dto -> Order entity
Mapper -> Entity : new Order(items, address)
activate Entity
Entity -> Entity : business rules\n(total > 0? address valid?)
note right of Entity
  **Entity / Domain Model.**
  The real business object. Holds rules
  + behavior. Knows nothing about HTTP
  or SQL. This is the valuable core.
end note
Entity --> Service : valid Order
deactivate Entity

Service -> RepoPort : save(order)
note right of RepoPort
  Service depends on the **interface**,
  not the DB. At runtime the injected
  Impl runs (Dependency Injection).
end note
RepoPort -> RepoImpl : (actual implementation)
activate RepoImpl
RepoImpl -> DB : INSERT INTO orders ...
DB --> RepoImpl : new id
RepoImpl --> Service : saved Order (with id)
deactivate RepoImpl

== response travels UP (inside -> outside) ==
Service -> Mapper : Order entity -> Response DTO
Mapper --> Service : OrderResponse
Service --> Router : OrderResponse
deactivate Service
Router -->> UI : 201 Created  { id, status }
deactivate Router
UI -->> User : shows "Order placed ✔"

note over User, DB
  DOWN: each layer adapts the request a little closer to the core.
  UP:   each layer adapts the result a little closer to the wire.
  DTOs guard the OUTER boundary; Entities live in the INNER core;
  the Mapper is the translator between the two worlds.
end note

@enduml
